@*
    The platform page, at "/platform".

    WHY IT EXISTS. Every claim this project makes is measured and written down, and all of it lives
    in markdown on GitHub. A reviewer with the live link and ten minutes never sees any of it. This
    page is the one place the evidence is reachable from the demo itself — so the answer to "is any
    of this real?" is a link in the footer rather than a repository tour.

    THE FIRST-PAINT RULE HOLDS HERE TOO, AND THIS PAGE IS THE PROOF OF IT. Nothing on it is fetched.
    It renders from the same static catalog snapshot the shelves render from, so it paints while the
    API and the database are asleep — which is exactly the architectural claim its first section
    makes. A platform page that woke the backend to boast about not needing the backend would be
    the funniest possible bug, so the rule is enforced by there being no HttpClient injected here at
    all.

    WHICH NUMBERS ARE LIVE AND WHICH ARE WRITTEN DOWN. The catalog counts come from the snapshot the
    browser already holds: they cannot go stale, because they ARE the data. Everything else is a
    measurement with a date beside it and a link to the document that produced it. That distinction
    is drawn visibly rather than quietly, because a page of hardcoded figures dressed as telemetry
    is worse than no page.

    WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE: a live cost readout. docs/PLAN.md asks for "the current month's
    cost", which would need an Azure billing credential inside a public demo's container. The cost
    section states the architecture that makes the bill zero and links the reasoning instead. An
    approximate number presented as live would be the same mistake this page exists to avoid.
*@
@page "/platform"
@inject CatalogService Catalog

<PageTitle>How this is built · Vela Commerce</PageTitle>

<nav class="vc-crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
    <a class="vc-crumbs__link" href="">Catalog</a>
    <span class="vc-crumbs__sep" aria-hidden="true">/</span>
    <span>Platform</span>
</nav>

<div class="vc-hero">
    <span class="vc-eyebrow">Platform &middot; the engineering behind the shop</span>
    <h1 class="vc-section-title">How this is built, and what it cost to prove</h1>
    <p class="vc-lede">
        A portfolio shop is easy to make claims about and hard to make true. Everything below was
        measured against this deployment or is enforced by a test that fails the build. Where a
        number came from a document, the document is linked; where something is not built, it says
        so.
    </p>
</div>

@* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   1. The architectural claim, demonstrated by the page the reader is currently looking at.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- *@
<section class="vc-plat-section">
    <h2 class="vc-plat-h2">One rule the whole build is shaped around</h2>

    <p class="vc-plat-body">
        <strong>Browsing never touches the API.</strong> The catalog, search, filtering and sorting
        all run in your browser against a static snapshot fetched once. The server is needed to put
        something in a cart, and not before.
    </p>

    <p class="vc-plat-body">
        That is not an optimisation, it is the hosting argument. The container runs at
        <em>zero replicas</em> when nobody is looking, so the first person to open a cart pays a
        real cold start &mdash; and the first person to <em>look</em> pays nothing, because nothing
        behind this page had to be awake to render it.
    </p>

    @if (Catalog.Snapshot is { } snapshot)
    {
        <div class="vc-plat-live">
            <span class="vc-plat-live__dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            <p class="vc-plat-live__text">
                The snapshot your browser is holding right now:
                <span class="vc-tabular">@snapshot.ProductCount</span> products,
                <span class="vc-tabular">@snapshot.VariantCount</span> SKUs, generated
                deterministically from seed <span class="vc-tabular">@snapshot.RandomSeed</span>.
                These three figures are read from that file rather than written on this page, so
                they cannot disagree with the shop.
            </p>
        </div>
    }
</section>

@* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   2. The measurements. Every row has a date and a source, and the two that are not from a
      document say where they are enforced instead.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- *@
<section class="vc-plat-section">
    <h2 class="vc-plat-h2">Measured, not asserted</h2>

    <p class="vc-plat-body">
        Each row is a number somebody could check. The cold start is the one that costs a visitor
        something, so it is first, and it is quoted at the tail rather than only at the median.
    </p>

    <div class="vc-plat-table-wrap">
        <table class="vc-plat-table">
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="col">What</th>
                    <th scope="col">Measured</th>
                    <th scope="col">Where it came from</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                @foreach (var row in Measurements)
                {
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row">@row.Label</th>
                        <td class="vc-tabular vc-plat-table__value">@row.Value</td>
                        <td>
                            <a class="vc-plat-link" href="@row.Href" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
                                @row.Source
                            </a>
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                }
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <p class="vc-plat-note">
        Two of these carry a correction rather than just a number. The mutation score moved two
        points on a commit that changed no test at all &mdash; the tool had reported kills for
        changes nothing in the suite could detect &mdash; and then barely moved when three real gaps
        were fixed. It is kept as a floor the build must hold, not quoted as a result.
    </p>

    <p class="vc-plat-note">
        The cold start was retracted twice before it settled. The first published figure came from
        three samples and called the spread &ldquo;suspiciously stable&rdquo;; five more arrived and
        one of them was five seconds slower than the rest. The correction is still visible in the
        document rather than tidied away, because a measurement that quietly changed is worth less
        than one that shows its working.
    </p>
</section>

@* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   3. Cost. No live figure, and the reason is stated rather than left as a gap.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- *@
<section class="vc-plat-section">
    <h2 class="vc-plat-h2">What it costs to leave running</h2>

    <p class="vc-plat-body">
        The target was a demo that is still up years from now without anyone paying attention to it,
        which ruled out every free tier that expires. Azure Container Apps grants every subscription
        180,000 vCPU-seconds a month as an ongoing allowance rather than a trial credit, and Neon's
        free plan has no expiry either.
    </p>

    <ul class="vc-plat-list">
        <li>
            <strong>The container scales to zero.</strong> One replica held warm would burn roughly
            3.7&times; the entire monthly allowance before a single visitor arrived. That single
            setting is the difference between nothing and roughly $48&ndash;120 a year.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>No VNet, no load balancer, no Log Analytics.</strong> Each is one line that every
            reference architecture recommends, and each bills at zero traffic forever &mdash; the
            networking pair alone is about $306 a year of standing rent.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Nothing polls the database on a timer.</strong> Neon's free plan allows 100
            compute-hours a month; a fifteen-minute reconciler poll would spend 61&ndash;75 of them
            before anybody visited. The health endpoint the platform probes deliberately does not
            touch the database.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <p class="vc-plat-note">
        There is no live cost readout on this page. Showing one would mean putting an Azure billing
        credential inside a public demo, and an approximate figure presented as current would be the
        exact species of claim the rest of this page exists to avoid.
    </p>
</section>

@* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   4. The decisions, which are the part worth arguing with.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- *@
<section class="vc-plat-section">
    <h2 class="vc-plat-h2">Decisions a reviewer might read as mistakes</h2>

    <p class="vc-plat-body">
        Eleven decision records, each naming the alternative it rejected and the test or file that
        now enforces it. These four are the ones most likely to look wrong at first glance.
    </p>

    <ul class="vc-plat-cards">
        @foreach (var record in Decisions)
        {
            <li class="vc-plat-card">
                <a class="vc-plat-card__link" href="@record.Href" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
                    <span class="vc-plat-card__num vc-tabular">@record.Number</span>
                    <span class="vc-plat-card__title">@record.Title</span>
                </a>
                <p class="vc-plat-card__body">@record.Why</p>
            </li>
        }
    </ul>

    <p class="vc-plat-body">
        <a class="vc-plat-link" href="https://github.com/jdoan5/vela-commerce/tree/main/docs/adr"
           target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">All eleven records</a>
        &middot;
        <a class="vc-plat-link" href="https://github.com/jdoan5/vela-commerce/tree/main/docs/measurements"
           target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">All four measurements</a>
        &middot;
        <a class="vc-plat-link" href="@(Repo)docs/at-100x-traffic.md"
           target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">What I&rsquo;d do at 100&times; traffic</a>
    </p>
</section>

@* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   5. The honesty section. It is last because it is the one a reader remembers, and it is
      not softened.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- *@
<section class="vc-plat-section">
    <h2 class="vc-plat-h2">What is not built</h2>

    <p class="vc-plat-body">
        A list of gaps is more useful than a list of features, because the features are visible on
        the page above and the gaps are not.
    </p>

    <dl class="vc-plat-gaps">
        <dt>Nothing watches this demo</dt>
        <dd>
            No uptime monitor, no heartbeat, no alerting of any kind. If it goes down, it is noticed
            on a monthly manual pass. That is the honest description of this project's availability
            story, and the fix is designed but not built.
        </dd>

        <dt>No database backup</dt>
        <dd>
            Deliberate, and recorded as a decision rather than skipped. The database holds a schema
            that is committed, a catalog generated from a committed seed, and demo rows that expire
            after a day &mdash; so a backup would preserve two things already in version control and
            one thing the system deletes on purpose. A rebuild from empty was timed instead, at
            2.59 s.
        </dd>

        <dt>The payments are a simulator</dt>
        <dd>
            There is no Stripe account and no real money. The simulator signs its own webhooks with
            HMAC-SHA256 and the receiver verifies them in constant time, so the settlement path,
            the duplicate-delivery handling and the idempotency are all real &mdash; only the money
            is imaginary.
        </dd>

        <dt>No per-PR preview environments, and no distributed tracing</dt>
        <dd>
            Both were planned. Neither exists. The plan is kept unedited in the repository beside a
            table of every place the build diverged from it, which is where these are listed.
        </dd>
    </dl>
</section>

@code {
    // Awaiting the same shared load task the header and the footer await: one fetch for the whole
    // app, and this page re-renders with the live counts once it completes. Note what this is NOT:
    // a call to the API. It is the static snapshot, which is the entire point of the first section.
    protected override Task OnInitializedAsync() => Catalog.EnsureLoadedAsync();

    private const string Repo = "https://github.com/jdoan5/vela-commerce/blob/main/";

    private sealed record Measurement(string Label, string Value, string Source, string Href);

    // Every value here is a written-down measurement, which is why every row carries its source.
    // They are const rather than fetched because fetching them would break the rule this page is
    // about; the cost of that choice is that they have to be updated by hand when they change, and
    // the repository's own discipline is that a number which stopped being true is a bug.
    private static readonly Measurement[] Measurements =
    [
        new("Cold start, first request after scale-to-zero",
            "32.14 s p50 · 37.45 s p95 · n=8",
            "cold-start.md",
            Repo + "docs/measurements/cold-start.md"),

        new("Warm, the request after that",
            "0.17 s p50 · 0.28 s p95",
            "cold-start.md",
            Repo + "docs/measurements/cold-start.md"),

        new("Rebuild from an empty database",
            "2.59 s",
            "restore-drill.md",
            Repo + "docs/measurements/restore-drill.md"),

        new("Container image",
            "206 MB",
            "cold-start.md",
            Repo + "docs/measurements/cold-start.md"),

        new("Tests, against real PostgreSQL 18 in containers",
            "414",
            "ci.yml — the build fails without them",
            Repo + ".github/workflows/ci.yml"),

        new("Line coverage over the three production assemblies",
            "68.2% · floor 65%",
            "coverage.runsettings — says what is counted",
            Repo + "coverage.runsettings"),

        new("Mutation score on the domain — read the caveat",
            "73.9% · floor 70%",
            "mutation-testing.md — why this number misleads",
            Repo + "docs/measurements/mutation-testing.md"),

        new("Trigram index chosen by the planner at",
            "30,000–100,000 rows",
            "trigram-search.md",
            Repo + "docs/measurements/trigram-search.md"),
    ];

    private sealed record Decision(string Number, string Title, string Why, string Href);

    private static readonly Decision[] Decisions =
    [
        new("0004",
            "The admin cannot ship an order or adjust stock",
            "It looks like a missing feature. It is a boundary: the demo admin is bound to one "
            + "visitor's session, and letting it move the shared stock ledger would let any stranger "
            + "empty the shop for everybody.",
            Repo + "docs/adr/0004-the-admin-cannot-ship-or-restock.md"),

        new("0006",
            "Races are decided by the database, never by a SELECT that ran first",
            "Reading a row, deciding in C#, then writing is the shape of every oversell bug. Every "
            + "contested write here is a conditional UPDATE or a unique index, and the loser learns "
            + "it from a row count.",
            Repo + "docs/adr/0006-the-database-decides-races.md"),

        new("0007",
            "The tenancy filter fails closed",
            "The predicate is 'there is a session AND the row belongs to it', so a request with no "
            + "session sees nothing rather than everything. The failure direction of a bug is an "
            + "empty cart, never somebody else's.",
            Repo + "docs/adr/0007-the-tenancy-filter-fails-closed.md"),

        new("0010",
            "Demo data expires on visits, not on a cron",
            "The plan called for a nightly job. This container is asleep unless somebody visited — "
            + "so the rows and the sweep have the same cause, and the scheduled version needed a "
            + "second image that nothing in the pipeline could update.",
            Repo + "docs/adr/0010-the-purge-runs-on-visits-not-on-a-clock.md"),
    ];
}
